#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106925} :activity/id #uuid "d489d7ad-a521-5b24-bbbd-5997d33ba8e3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049335 :topic/id #uuid "ecb1b06f-16f7-5312-9cad-31f797ed7b6f" :topic/math-academy-id 1287} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107078 :tutorial/content "Geometrically, the absolute value of the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ represents the volume enclosed by the parallelepiped whose sides are formed by the three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the parallelepiped shown below. The sides of this parallelepiped are formed by the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume of this parallelepiped is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV = |\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})|\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5917536-1cb1-5fba-9f81-7d866e832dcc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2956 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "be7df47b-44a5-5a3f-b532-2fc81385da20" :step/math-academy-id 464354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105907 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105596 :question/id #uuid "b3be2b13-b455-5dde-accf-259c075e1e1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe9711f6-e2ad-5eed-8ded-a6153b0322bd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050356}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484248}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Volume of a Parallelepiped Spanned by Three Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "7d16dd58-a46a-51e5-8c79-4e7cf4e9b6c9" :step/math-academy-id 464355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107079 :tutorial/content "Three vectors are said to be **coplanar** if they are parallel to the same plane.\n\nWhen we have three coplanar vectors, the parallelepiped they span is *flat* on the plane, and therefore its volume is zero. Consequently, the triple product of the vectors is zero as well.\n\nSo, if three vectors are coplanar, then their triple product is zero. The converse is also true: if the triple product of three nonzero vectors is zero, then the vectors are coplanar.\n\nTherefore, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar $\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad$    $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3012c88-77aa-5271-ad5f-b2176f99ba15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2957 :tutorial/title "The Volume Spanned by Coplanar Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "04e9769b-9cba-55d2-a3b5-ab09464bb0b3" :step/math-academy-id 464358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105908 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105597 :question/id #uuid "c6f2dacc-11b0-5689-97d9-e1869c64aae1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4351"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26c84e5b-97a7-5116-a90c-e4f011e87cf0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532965} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532964}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Three Vectors are Coplanar"} :step/id #uuid "58a570ee-c7cc-5be3-bfd4-c8e0901c10f3" :step/math-academy-id 464359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107085 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we are not given the vectors that represent the sides of a parallelepiped. Instead, we might be given the points at which the vertices lie.\n\nTo compute the volume of a parallelepiped given the vertices, we can still use a similar process as before. The only difference is that we have to start by computing the displacement vectors between the points.\n\nOnce we have the displacement vectors, then we can find the desired volume by computing the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nLet's see an example of this." :tutorial/id #uuid "e078c251-f4ae-5e91-abd6-3272b43e7b22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3310 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Volume of a Parallelepiped Given the Coordinates of the Vertices"} :step/id #uuid "f33f722f-f69e-5a75-bfb3-339e2cb1e5bf" :step/math-academy-id 464362 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105909 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105598 :question/id #uuid "4deee6cc-69e4-5da4-9c78-57bf6c988500" :question/math-academy-id "e-4352"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c790ad-c7fb-596c-8b60-cddd9b4e2165" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459292}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Volume of a Parallelepiped Spanned by Three Position Vectors Given Their Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "af06265a-9f99-50db-9f20-6b0f473380d3" :step/math-academy-id 464363 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107084 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact that the volume of a parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV =  |\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})|\n$$\n\nBut why is this formula true? To understand why, remember that the volume of a parallelepiped can be obtained by multiplying the area of the base by the height.\n\nAs we can see in the diagram below, \n\n- the area of the base is given by $A = | \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} |,$ and\n\n- the height is given by $h = |\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}}\\:\\mathbf{a}|.$\n\n![](images/cf/cfc492127bed6d6929518a708288691c5fe79e1842be6ecf92666bed6b03200b.png)\n\nTo compute the volume, we will multiply the area of the base by the height. First, though, let's write down the expression for the height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =|{\\text{proj}}_{b\\times c}\\,a| \\\\  & =|\\frac{a\\cdot (b\\times c)}{|b\\times c|}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|}{|b\\times c|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying the area of the base by the height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =A\\cdot h \\\\  & =|b\\times c|\\cdot \\frac{|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|}{|b\\times c|} \\\\  & =|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6b371cde-1beb-5aa2-b564-0bae79e2faf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3309 :tutorial/title "Proof That the Volume of a Parallelepiped is Given by the Absolute Value of the Triple Product"} :step/id #uuid "e1974a4e-dd77-5808-a604-05fb1fa36604" :step/math-academy-id 464366}] :activity/title "Volumes of Parallelepipeds" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105964}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494034} :activity/id #uuid "3f085bc3-d4e1-5959-bd6a-cbbb58662438" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049335 :topic/id #uuid "ecb1b06f-16f7-5312-9cad-31f797ed7b6f" :topic/math-academy-id 1287} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6122a148-198a-57cc-b7dd-7fcbcaac6bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293328} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6bff096-9adc-55e6-b402-42dc714742c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c74fea15-7593-5e2d-a899-51b33896c8fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-281030" :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(-1,0,1),$ $B(2,-3,4),$ $C(-2,-2,2),$ and $D(0,-1,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-3j+4k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =3i-3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i-2j+2k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =-i-2j+1k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(-j+5k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =i-j+4k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|-(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(-8+1)+3(-4-1)+3(1-(-2)) \\\\  & =-21-15+9 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-27|=27.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2818fbd2-4e8e-5ddd-8195-a9f944008965"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a627e562-14bd-588f-a5f4-1d17e6cb6828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493653} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d8c1a0-222c-5144-9f47-a1a87a86c092" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b407ea24-4cac-5cb8-bbfb-b1a7b706dc73" :question/math-academy-id "q-280770" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $x=⟨1,k,2⟩$, $y=⟨5,-2,3⟩$, and $z=⟨-4,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ k & 1\\end{aligned}|-k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot k)-k(5\\cdot 1-3\\cdot -4)+2(5k-(-2)(-4)) \\\\  & =-2-3k-k(5+12)+2(5k-8) \\\\  & =-2-3k-17k+10k-16 \\\\  & =-10k-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ -10k-18 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{9}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7627e8da-c0a2-598b-b7d9-ae6f8ff09f19" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf3c4e57-2ad8-56ca-9a27-7faa2aaf1255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102687} {:answer/id #uuid "d07f6bfb-5502-5a87-b026-3ef84ecd878d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102688} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1ae10b-c98c-5aea-b28d-a0931aaf66da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102689} {:answer/id #uuid "4b15e493-3d4c-50d7-8e36-9379740a5e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102690} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8d1f64-85e4-551c-8f55-38866b9af8a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102688} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8e5f2b-038e-5f94-8231-636fcf8c722e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c6409f39-73a4-56e7-9d4f-f576ba58985d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20138" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(1,2,-1)$, $B(2,3,1)$, $C(4,1,2)$, and $D(1,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =3i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)-(3\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 3)+2(3\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-9-9+12 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-6|=6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9044800c-37a9-5a39-94d5-148ee640cb1e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc5ee62-4f20-5c54-9e5a-bbb471927bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493468} {:answer/id #uuid "3135cadf-898b-5d47-89f0-df3060a008eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493473} {:answer/id #uuid "389cf47a-7d3c-5e4e-b0ed-207e5d8a0087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493485} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcfd0be-c665-50f6-b594-87f4f9162f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493643} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7cc567-6679-5581-b389-e82348eb1ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493668}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493468} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b436b69-27e7-557e-9669-3a80b22007ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b5a6dbe-bec1-5af9-82e5-d252aa38eb1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73733" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨-1,1,1⟩$, $b=⟨2,1,0⟩$, and $c=⟨k,-2,-3⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ k & -3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 0)-(2\\cdot (-3)-k\\cdot 0)+(2\\cdot (-2)-k\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6-4-k \\\\  & =5-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 5-k & =0 \\\\ k & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=5.$"} :step/id #uuid "9453de60-3efa-5f31-981a-06a36470b7cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196c7bb9-2703-5d83-8058-24be71bcb43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260097} {:answer/id #uuid "44662d03-7907-5288-b2f2-17f084b58313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260471} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8121de-a71a-5f79-841e-03f4c4d8f85c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260679} {:answer/id #uuid "c69b8832-1644-5736-8a79-f96a8d866802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261625} {:answer/id #uuid "fba18045-5247-5864-b09c-6d2f59aad9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260471} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e3b359-0ccd-5626-ac16-03a3053097e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1edaed17-510c-5349-a80b-092dc57c864f" :question/math-academy-id "q-128475" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(7,6,4),$ $B(8,5,9),$ $C(7,4,2),$ and $D(5,7,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(8i+5j+9k)-(7i+6j+4k) \\\\  & =i-j+5k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,5⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(7i+4j+2k)-(7i+6j+4k) \\\\  & =-2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(5i+7j+4k)-(7i+6j+4k) \\\\  & =-2i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-2))+(0\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-2))+5(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2-4-20 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-22|=22.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e6521478-5710-54f7-9cae-ff12b96891b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494014}}] :activity/title "Volumes of Parallelepipeds" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493373}]}
